@startuml
' Communication diagram — the same opening, arranged by the objects
' rather than by the time
' Sources: ../../../analysis/use-case-descriptions.md (UC-01), Rule F-2, Rule A-3
'
' A sequence diagram answers "in what order". This one answers "between
' whom": which objects know about the file, and which are only told.

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam componentStyle rectangle
skinparam component {
  BackgroundColor White
  BorderColor #333
  ArrowColor #333
}

[Browser] as BR
[Extension\n(background)] as EXT
[The reader\n(server.py)] as SRV
[Workspace] as WS
[State\n(localStorage)] as ST
[Tab] as TAB
[Frame] as FRM
[File's page] as PAGE
[Engine] as ENG

BR --> EXT : the tab's address changed
EXT --> SRV : GET /?open=<path>
SRV --> BR : the workspace
BR --> WS : the page runs
WS --> SRV : GET /api/tree?path=<folder>
SRV --> WS : the names inside it
WS --> ST : the folder it stands in
WS --> TAB : opens the file
TAB --> FRM : the page in a frame
FRM --> SRV : GET /view/<path>?embed=1
SRV --> FRM : the file's own page
FRM --> ENG : the page runs the engine
ENG --> SRV : GET /f/<path> (the bytes)
SRV --> ENG : the bytes
ENG --> FRM : the document
FRM --> BR : the note is on the screen

note bottom of WS
  F-2: the workspace reveals the file in
  the tree — the folders on the way to it
  are opened, so the file is shown where
  it lives.
end note

note bottom of TAB
  A-3: a tab is the reader's own page in
  a frame. The workspace composes; it
  does not draw a document.
end note

note bottom of ST
  The state has one owner, and it is the
  browser's storage (A-4).
end note
@enduml
